or a chow.\n\nAnother consideration is the unshown tiles. For example, if a tile has very few left, discarding it might be risky because someone might need it. For example, F3 has 1 left. If I discard F3, there's only one left, so someone might take it. But if I discard a tile with many left, like W2 (unshown has 3 left), it's less risky.\n\nSo, considering all this, the best move might be to play T7, as it's part of a potential chow and has available tiles in the unshown. Or play B4, which is part of a chow someone else has but might still be useful for me.\n\nWait, the valid actions include Play T7 and Play B4. Let's check:\n\nIf I play T7, then I have T7 (1) left. If I later draw T8 or T9, I can form a chow. That's a possibility.\n\nIf I play B4, since B4 is part of a chow (B4-B5-B6), and B5 is already out, so B4 might not be needed by others. Plus, B4 has 3 left in the unshown, so playing it is safe.\n\nAlternatively, playing W2. W2 has 3 left in the unshown, so it's safe to discard.\n\nBetween these options, which one gives the best chance to form a winning hand. Since the current hand has no chows, the best bet is to discard a tile that allows for a potential chow. So playing T7 or B4.\n\nBut B4 is part of a chow that's already been made by the player across, so maybe B4 is less needed. T7 could be part of a chow that I can form if I draw T8 or T9.\n\nTherefore, playing T7 might be the better choice.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has three pairs (W2, T2, B2) but lacks chows or pungs. Discarding T7 allows flexibility to form a T7-T8-T9 chow if needed, leveraging available T8/T9 in unshown tiles.]\nReason: [T7 is part of a potential chow (T7-T8-T9) with available tiles (T8:3, T9:2). Discarding it maintains concealed hand value (2 points) and opens opportunities for chows or Seven Pairs if needed.]\nAnswer: Play T7"}
